<main class="layout-container layout-stack-main">

  <header class="surface-bento page-hero-bento">
    <h1 class="text-page-title">Steep Bluffs, Landslides, Floodplains &amp; Coal Mines</h1>
    <p class="text-body page-hero-lead">
      Navigating critical areas across King and Snohomish counties: coastal bluff drainage management, FEMA floodplain insurance rules, Elevation Certificates, and East King County historical coal mine overlays.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <h2 class="text-widget-title">The Golden Rule of Slope Hazards: Water Is the Primary Enemy</h2>
      <p class="text-body">
        The stunning terrain of Western Washington comes with distinct geological considerations. Landslide risks on Puget Sound bluffs or steep ravines are almost always triggered by uncontrolled surface runoff (like leaking gutters, misdirected downspouts, or removing deep-rooted native trees). 
      </p>
      <p class="text-body">
        If you are considering a home near a steep slope or in a river valley, check official municipal GIS mapping early. For river valley properties, verifying base flood elevation and obtaining an official Elevation Certificate can often cut annual FEMA flood insurance premiums dramatically.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Steep Slopes, Coastal Bluffs &amp; Critical Area (ECA) Rules</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Properties along Puget Sound coastal bluffs (Edmonds, Mukilteo, Shoreline, West Seattle) or inland ravine slopes carry special local zoning designations called Environmentally Critical Areas (ECA) or Geologically Hazardous Areas.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-3">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Building Limits</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Setback Buffers</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Municipal codes generally require a 15 to 50-foot building setback from the top or toe of any slope exceeding 40 percent grade to preserve structural integrity and prevent loading.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Natural Reinforcement</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Vegetation Management</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          Tree roots act as natural soil rebar. Cutting down mature trees on a bluff face or steep slope can rapidly destabilize topsoil layers and increase slide potential during wet winter months.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Hydrology Control</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Stormwater Tightlines</h3>
        </header>
        <p class="text-body">
          All roof runoff, driveway drainage, and sump pump discharges must be piped securely inside solid PVC tightlines to approved dissipation zones at the bottom of the slope or municipal storm systems.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">River Floodplains: FEMA Zones &amp; Elevation Certificates</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Major river basins across Snohomish and King counties (including the Snohomish, Stillaguamish, Snoqualmie, and Skykomish rivers) contain mapped 100-year floodplains.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-1">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">FEMA Designation</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Zone A / AE (100-Year Floodplain)</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Conventional Loan Impact</span>
            <p class="text-body">Mandatory federal flood insurance is required by conventional lenders throughout the life of the mortgage.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Homeowner Mitigation Strategy</span>
            <p class="text-body">Obtain a licensed surveyor Elevation Certificate to verify the exact structure height above Base Flood Elevation (BFE) to negotiate lowest possible premiums.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Foundation Engineering</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Crawl Space Construction</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Conventional Loan Impact</span>
            <p class="text-body">Underwriting teams will review foundation blueprints for hydrostatic pressure relief systems to ensure floodwaters do not collapse stem walls.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Homeowner Mitigation Strategy</span>
            <p class="text-body">Install engineered breakaway flood vents, maintaining a ratio of 1 square inch of vent per 1 square foot of enclosed crawl space area.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <header class="text-center">
          <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Exemption Pathways</div>
          <h3 class="text-widget-title">Letter of Map Amendment (LOMA)</h3>
        </header>

        <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Conventional Loan Impact</span>
            <p class="text-body">Successfully filing a LOMA officially removes the property from the Special Flood Hazard Area (SFHA), dropping the mandatory insurance requirement entirely.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="calc-field-group">
            <span class="calc-field-label">Homeowner Mitigation Strategy</span>
            <p class="text-body">File with FEMA alongside an Elevation Certificate if the natural ground immediately under the living structure sits higher than the surrounding flood plane.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">Historical Coal Mine Overlay Zones (East King County)</h2>
    <div class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <p class="text-body section-body-prose" style="text-align: left; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">
        During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, extensive underground coal mining took place across East King County communities, notably in <strong>Newcastle, Issaquah, Renton, and Coal Creek</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body section-body-prose" style="text-align: left; margin: 0;">
        King County tracks these historical shaft networks using Coal Mine Hazard Overlay Maps. While most deep shafts were permanently sealed decades ago, properties situated within mine hazard zones trigger mandatory seller disclosures on Form 17. Buyers should verify whether a home sits over shallow mine workings (higher subsidence risk), unsealed air shafts, or deep bedrock formations before proceeding.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

